<Window x:Class="Plith.DropCatcher.Shelf.ShelfWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:p="clr-namespace:Plith.Views.Presentation"
        xmlns:shelf="clr-namespace:Plith.DropCatcher.Shelf"
        WindowStyle="None"
        AllowsTransparency="True"
        Background="#01000000"
        Topmost="True"
        ShowInTaskbar="False"
        ShowActivated="True"
        ResizeMode="NoResize"
        Width="384" Height="283"
        Left="-32000" Top="-32000"
        Title="Plith shelf">

    <!-- The shelf page, as a window of its own, and a SIBLING of CatcherWindow rather than a
         mode of it. The two look almost identical here and a later reader will want to merge
         them, so the reason they cannot be one window is written down rather than left to be
         rediscovered:

           - CatcherWindow must never take activation. It appears under a pointer that is
             already holding a file, and pulling focus mid-drag is how a drag gets cancelled.
             It therefore carries WS_EX_NOACTIVATE and ShowActivated="False".
           - ShelfWindow must take activation. Esc, arrow keys and a visible selection have
             nothing to arrive at in a window that never takes focus, and Deactivated is this
             window's main dismissal.

         Those are opposite answers to the same question, and WS_EX_NOACTIVATE is applied to the
         HWND at SourceInitialized. One window cannot hold both, and a window that flipped the
         style between two modes would be flipping it exactly while a drag or a focus change was
         in flight. Two windows, one job each.

         Everything below that is the same as CatcherWindow is the same DELIBERATELY: layered
         with per-pixel alpha so the shape can grow out of the notch rather than cut into place,
         1% alpha on the background because WPF hit-tests a Transparent brush but not a null one,
         and the same off-screen Left/Top so the first frame is never painted at 0,0. The cost is
         the same too, and it is the reason this file is hard to check: a layered window cannot be
         captured over RDP by any means, so it can only be judged by looking at it. -->
    <Grid>
        <!-- ONE surface whose width, height and corner radius all come from a single expansion
             value, the same rule NotchGeometry exists to enforce for the notch. Aligned to the
             top-centre because the shelf grows out of the notch, which is part of the screen's
             top edge.

             ClipToBounds, because the page inside is held at its FULL size for the whole growth
             (see ApplyExpansion): re-laying the page out every frame would squeeze its tiles and
             read as a window being resized rather than a shape opening. -->
        <!-- INSET BY THE SHADOW MARGIN, and casting the same shadow Plith's panel does.

             This window is the notch's WINDOW now, not its panel: the page rect grown by
             NotchGeometry.PanelShadowMarginDip on the left, the right and the bottom, which is
             exactly what Plith's own window measures (384 by 178 around a 356 by 164 panel).
             Sent that way by ShelfSession.Open.

             Before this, the catcher's window was the panel alone and had no shadow, so the
             handover moved every edge by 14 DIP and dropped the shadow in the same frame. A
             person reported it as the notch closing and reopening in a fraction of a second, and
             it is the last visible seam in a swap that is otherwise covered by the ShelfShown
             acknowledgement.

             The shadow's numbers are copied from OsdContent.xaml rather than shared, because
             that is a XAML resource in the other project and this one cannot reach it: depth six
             straight down, blur twenty. Direction 270 is down in WPF's counter-clockwise-from-east
             convention, which matters because a surface flush with the top of the screen has
             nothing above it for a blur to fall on. -->
        <Border x:Name="Shape"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                VerticalAlignment="Top"
                Margin="{x:Static p:NotchGeometry.ShelfShapeMargin}"
                Width="356" Height="116"
                ClipToBounds="True"
                Background="#F0111111">
            <Border.Effect>
                <DropShadowEffect ShadowDepth="6" Direction="270" BlurRadius="20"
                                  Opacity="0.55" Color="Black" />
            </Border.Effect>
            <!-- The page fades in only after the shape has mostly settled, which is the whole
                 effect: the surface arrives first and the content arrives into it. Opacity is
                 driven from the same expansion value as the shape around it. -->
            <shelf:ShelfSurface x:Name="Page"
                                Opacity="0"
                                HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                VerticalAlignment="Top" />
        </Border>
    </Grid>
</Window>
